---
title: PETScDiffEq Lotka-Volterra Work-Precision Diagrams
author: Harsh Singh
---

These pages put the time integrators of [PETSc's TS](https://petsc.org/release/manual/ts/),
called through [PETScDiffEq.jl](https://github.com/SciML/PETScDiffEq.jl), next to
OrdinaryDiffEq and Sundials on four problems: this non-stiff ODE, the stiff HIRES problem,
the Robertson DAE and a 1-D Brusselator with a sparse Jacobian. Each point is one pair of
tolerances, with the mean absolute error at the final time against a reference solution and
the fastest of up to 20 solves, as `WorkPrecisionSet` measures them.

Solid lines with circles are PETScDiffEq, dashed lines with squares are OrdinaryDiffEq or
Sundials, and lines of one color are the same method or its nearest counterpart. Two pairs
here share their coefficients: `TSRK("3bs")` with `BS3` and `TSRK("5dp")` with `DP5`.
`TSRK("7vr")` and `TSRK("8vr")` are Verner pairs, but not the ones in `Vern7` and `Vern8`.

```julia
using OrdinaryDiffEqLowOrderRK, OrdinaryDiffEqTsit5, OrdinaryDiffEqVerner, Sundials
using SciMLLogging
include(joinpath(@__DIR__, "wpd_common.jl"))
gr()

function lotka_volterra!(du, u, p, t)
    du[1] = 1.5 * u[1] - u[1] * u[2]
    du[2] = -3.0 * u[2] + u[1] * u[2]
    return nothing
end

prob = ODEProblem(lotka_volterra!, [1.0, 1.0], (0.0, 10.0))
```

## Reference solution

`Vern9` at a tolerance of 1e-14, checked against `Vern8` at the same tolerance.

```julia
ref = reference(prob, Vern9(); tol = 1.0e-14)
check = reference(prob, Vern8(); tol = 1.0e-14)
reference_gap = maximum(abs, ref.u[end] - check.u[end])
```

## Matched coefficients

With a fixed step of 0.01 each pair takes the same 1000 steps, so if the coefficients match
the final states agree to rounding:

```julia
for (pair, a, b) in (
        ("TSRK(\"3bs\"), BS3", TSRK("3bs"), BS3()), ("TSRK(\"5dp\"), DP5", TSRK("5dp"), DP5()),
    )
    @printf("%-18s %.2e\n", pair, fixed_step_gap(prob, a, b; dt = 0.01))
end
```

## Work-precision diagram

`abstol` runs from 1e-6 to 1e-13 and `reltol` from 1e-3 to 1e-10.

```julia
abstols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (6:13)
reltols = 1.0 ./ 10.0 .^ (3:10)
setups = [
    Dict(:alg => TSRK("3bs")), Dict(:alg => BS3()),
    Dict(:alg => TSRK("5dp")), Dict(:alg => DP5()),
    Dict(:alg => TSRK("7vr")), Dict(:alg => Vern7()),
    Dict(:alg => TSRK("8vr")), Dict(:alg => Vern8()),
    Dict(:alg => Tsit5()), Dict(:alg => CVODE_Adams()),
]
names = [
    "TSRK(\"3bs\")", "BS3", "TSRK(\"5dp\")", "DP5", "TSRK(\"7vr\")", "Vern7",
    "TSRK(\"8vr\")", "Vern8", "Tsit5", "CVODE_Adams",
]
colors = [1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 4, 4, 5, 6]
wp = WorkPrecisionSet(
    prob, abstols, reltols, setups; names, appxsol = ref, save_everystep = false,
    maxiters = 10^7, numruns = 20, verbose = SciMLLogging.None(),
)
wpd_plot(wp, colors; title = "Lotka-Volterra, non-stiff, 2 states")
```

```julia; wrap = false
pairs = [("TSRK(\"3bs\")", "BS3"), ("TSRK(\"5dp\")", "DP5")]
summarize(wp, 1.0e-8, pairs)
```

The two references agree to within 1e-13 at the final time, more than 100 times below the
smallest error plotted:

```julia
reference_margin(wp, reference_gap)
```

Every PETScDiffEq point includes a cost per solve that does not depend on the method or the
tolerance. A solve of a single step measures it, and at loose tolerances the PETScDiffEq times
level off a little above it:

```julia; wrap = false
one_step_table(
    [prob],
    [
        ("TSRK(\"3bs\")", TSRK("3bs"), 1), ("BS3", BS3(), 1),
        ("TSRK(\"5dp\")", TSRK("5dp"), 1), ("DP5", DP5(), 1),
        ("TSRK(\"8vr\")", TSRK("8vr"), 1), ("Vern8", Vern8(), 1),
    ],
)
```

Time to an error of 1e-8, interpolated in log-log between the measured points of each solver
that no other point of the same solver beats on both error and time. "At most" means the
solver's cheapest run was already below 1e-8.

```julia; wrap = false
time_to_error(wp, 1.0e-8)
```

`TSRK("5dp")` and `DP5` take nearly the same number of steps and end at nearly the same error,
so the gap between them is the cost per solve above and the cost of each step. The matched
pairs at the tightest tolerance, with the time per step attempt taken as the whole solve time
over the number of attempts:

```julia; wrap = false
compare_pairs(wp, pairs)
```

Every point:

```julia; wrap = false
print_points(wp)
```

